*LEAKED*

# Build junk that must never reach a PUBLIC repo (added 2026-08-21).
# .next/ was already ignored, but a renamed quarantine copy was not - 359 MB
# of stale July build output was about to be committed.
.next.quarantine-*/
build_out.txt
results/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GENERATED OUTPUT AND SCRATCH (added 2026-09-04 after a full untracked audit).
#
# The repo had 1,740 untracked entries and 1,566 of them - 90% - were generated
# output that must never reach a PUBLIC repo. github.com/SGDietz/aiASAP is
# public: anything committed here is world-readable.
#
# .next.quarantine-* was already ignored, but .next.corrupt-* is the identical
# class and was missed, so a dead 578-file corrupt build from 2026-09-03 was
# sitting one `git add -A` away from being published.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
artifacts/
.next.corrupt-*/
.next-installer-check/
.codex-qa/
work/
work-output/

# Point-in-time copies taken before an edit. For files that are UNTRACKED these
# are the only pre-change copy that exists, so they stay on disk - they are just
# never committed.
*.bak-*

# Codex's local scratch, not this project's.
.codex-floating.png
.codex-verify-floating.py

# ONE LOCKFILE ONLY (2026-09-04). package-lock.json is tracked and this project
# runs on npm. A second lockfile for a different package manager lets npm and
# pnpm disagree about the dependency tree, and lets a build resolve against
# whichever it finds first - a difference that shows up as a mystery at deploy
# time, not at commit time. The file stays on disk; it is simply never
# committed. If the project ever moves to pnpm, that is a deliberate switch:
# remove package-lock.json in the same change and drop this rule.
pnpm-lock.yaml
